 in response to expression of EVI1. In SCC-25 cells, 334 significantly up- and 417 significantly down-regulated genes were identified. 71 and 181 genes were up- and down-regulated, respectively, in response to EVI1 expression in both cell lines. Gene ontology analysis revealed, among others, the terms epithelium development, extracellular matrix organisationorganization, cell adhesion, response to growth factor, and epithelial cell proliferation to be significantly enriched among the 252 genes that were regulated by EVI1 in a consistent manner in both cell lines.","gpl":"16791","gse":"187454","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2022/06/01","suppfile":"CSV","samples":[{"accession":"GSM5668523","title":"RW01014: SCC-25_EVI1_rep2"},{"accession":"GSM5668512","title":"RW01003: CAL-33_vec_rep3"},{"accession":"GSM5668515","title":"RW01006: CAL-33_EVI1_rep2"},{"accession":"GSM5668511","title":"RW01002: CAL-33_vec_rep2"},{"accession":"GSM5668514","title":"RW01005: CAL-33_EVI1_rep1"},{"accession":"GSM5668517","title":"RW01008: CAL-33_EVI1_rep4"},{"accession":"GSM5668516","title":"RW01007: CAL-33_EVI1_rep3"},{"accession":"GSM5668520","title":"RW01011: SCC-25_vec_rep3"},{"accession":"GSM5668522","title":"RW01013: SCC-25_EVI1_rep1"},{"accession":"GSM5668513","title":"RW01004: CAL-33_vec_rep4"},{"accession":"GSM5668525","title":"RW01016: SCC-25_EVI1_rep4"},{"accession":"GSM5668519","title":"RW01010: SCC-25_vec_rep2"},{"accession":"GSM5668524","title":"RW01015: SCC-25_EVI1_rep3"},{"accession":"GSM5668518","title":"RW01009: SCC-25_vec_rep1"},{"accession":"GSM5668521","title":"RW01012: SCC-25_vec_rep4"},{"accession":"GSM5668510","title":"RW01001: CAL-33_vec_rep1"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[{"relationtype":"SRA","targetobject":"SRP344381","targetftplink":"ftp://ftp-trace.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sra/sra-instant/reads/ByStudy/sra/SRP/SRP344/SRP344381/"}],"n_samples":16,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["35162973"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE187nnn/GSE187454/","geo2r":"yes","bioproject":"PRJNA777573"},"200151128":{"uid":"200151128","accession":"GSE151128","gds":"","title":"RNA-seq analysis of SCC1 and FaDu cells after FOSL1 depletion","summary":"In order to examine how FOSL1 affects the global gene transcriptome in HNSCC, we performed RNA-sequencing in SCC1 and FaDu cells treated with FOSL1 siRNA. Depletion of FOSL1 led to inhibition of cancer stemness genes.","gpl":"21290","gse":"151128","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2022/05/23","suppfile":"XLSX","samples":[{"accession":"GSM4567063","title":"SCC1 FOSL1 depletion"},{"accession":"GSM4567064","title":"FaDu control"},{"accession":"GSM4567062","title":"SCC1 control"},{"accession":"GSM4567065","title":"FaDu FOSL1 depletion"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[{"relationtype":"SRA","targetobject":"SRP263452","targetftplink":"ftp://ftp-trace.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sra/sra-instant/reads/ByStudy/sra/SRP/SRP263/SRP263452/"}],"n_samples":4,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":[],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE151nnn/GSE151128/","geo2r":"yes","bioproject":"PRJNA634840"},"200198818":{"uid":"200198818","accession":"GSE198818","gds":"","title":"Long non-coding RNA ELDR promotes cell cycle progression in normal oral keratinocytes through induction of a CTCF-FOXM1-AURKA signaling axis","summary":"Long non-coding RNAs (lncRNAs) have gained widespread attention as a potential new layer of regulation in biological processes during development and disease. The lncRNA ELDR (EGFR long non‐coding downstream RNA) was recently shown to be highly expressed in oral cancers as compared to adjacent non-tumor tissue, and we previously reported that ELDR may be an oncogene as inhibition of ELDR reduces tumor growth in oral cancer models. Furthermore, overexpression of ELDR induces proliferation and colony formation in normal oral keratinocytes (NOKs). In this study, we examined in further detail how ELDR drives the neoplastic transformation of normal keratinocytes. We performed RNA-seq analysis on NOKs stably expressing ELDR (NOK-ELDR), which revealed that ELDR enhances the expression of cell cycle-related genes. Increased expression of Aurora kinase A (AURKA) and its downstream gene targets Polo-Like Kinase 1 (PLK1), Cell Division Cycle 25C (CDC25C), Cyclin-Dependent Kinase 1 (CDK1), and Cyclin B1 (CCNB1) are significantly increased in NOK-ELDR cells, suggesting induction of G2/M progression. We further identified CCCTC-Binding Factor (CTCF) as a binding partner of ELDR in NOK-ELDR cells. We showed that ELDR stabilizes CTCF and increases its expression. Finally, we demonstrate the ELDR-CTCF axis upregulates transcription factor Forkhead Box M1 (FOXM1), which induces AURKA expression and downstream G2/M transition. These findings provide mechanistic insights into the role of the lncRNA ELDR as a potential driver of oral cancer during neoplastic transformation of normal keratinocytes.","gpl":"24676","gse":"198818","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2022/05/11","suppfile":"TSV","samples":[{"accession":"GSM5957381","title":"ELDR over expressed (stable) [Exp A1]"},{"accession":"GSM5957382","title":"ELDR over expressed (stable) [Exp A2]"},{"accession":"GSM5957379","title":"Control 2"},{"accession":"GSM5957383","title":"ELDR over expressed (stable) [Exp A3]"},{"accession":"GSM5957380","title":"Control 3"},{"accession":"GSM5957378","title":"Control 1"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":6,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platform