ov/sra/sra-instant/reads/ByStudy/sra/SRP/SRP304/SRP304715/"}],"n_samples":2297,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["35508656"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE166nnn/GSE166148/","geo2r":"no","bioproject":"PRJNA699478"},"200195850":{"uid":"200195850","accession":"GSE195850","gds":"","title":"CCT2 promotes the proliferation and migration of head and neck squamous cell carcinoma via activating PI3K/AKT pathway","summary":"Purpose: RNA-seq was used to evaluate the effect of CCT2 knockdown in HNSCC cell lines. Methods: CCT2 was knocked down in SAS cell lines through shRNA and then RNA was extracted from control cells and knockdown cells. Results: Using an optimized data analysis workflow, we identified 205 differentially expressed genes (DEG), of which 141 genes were down-regulated and 64 genes up-regulated, respectively (fold change >2 and adjust p-value < 0.05). RT-qPCR was used to confirm the reliability of RNA-seq data. KEGG and GSEA analysis indicated that PI3K/AKT signaling pathway might be involved in the function of CCT2, which had been proved by western blotting.","gpl":"30209","gse":"195850","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2022/02/03","suppfile":"TXT","samples":[{"accession":"GSM5852130","title":"SAS cells, shCCT2_3"},{"accession":"GSM5852127","title":"SAS cells, shCtrl_2"},{"accession":"GSM5852128","title":"SAS cells, shCCT2_1"},{"accession":"GSM5852126","title":"SAS cells, shCtrl_1"},{"accession":"GSM5852129","title":"SAS cells, shCCT2_2"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":5,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":[],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE195nnn/GSE195850/","geo2r":"yes","bioproject":"PRJNA802278"},"200189290":{"uid":"200189290","accession":"GSE189290","gds":"","title":"Differentially expressed genes in head and neck cancer cell line (Sa3), control Sa3 and miR-30e-3p transfected Sa3","summary":"Differentially expressed genes in head and neck cancer cell line (Sa3), control Sa3 and miR-30e-3p transfected Sa3","gpl":"21185","gse":"189290","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by array","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2022/01/16","suppfile":"TXT, XLSX","samples":[{"accession":"GSM5697080","title":"Sa3_vs_Sa3_miR-30e-3p"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":1,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["35327465","35409173"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE189nnn/GSE189290/","geo2r":"yes","bioproject":"PRJNA782360"},"200193205":{"uid":"200193205","accession":"GSE193205","gds":"","title":"ΔNp63-Senataxin circuit controls keratinocyte differentiation by promoting the transcriptional termination of epidermal genes","summary":"ΔNp63, a master regulator of epithelial biology, is involved in regulating epithelial stem cell function, maintaining the integrity of stratified epithelial cells and committing epidermal cells to the differentiation program. To this end, ΔNp63 exploits several direct mechanisms. Here, we elucidated a novel mechanism whereby ΔNp63 efficiently sustains the expression of epidermal differentiation genes. We show that ΔNp63 interacts with Senataxin (SETX), an RNA/DNA helicase able to resolve the R-loop intermediates over the GC-rich termination sites of coding genes. Notably, we found that SETX and ΔNp63 co-regulate a subset of genes involved in the early step of the keratinocyte differentiation program. At the molecular level, SETX physically binds the p63 DNA binding motifs present in two early epidermal differentiation genes, Keratin 1 (KRT1) and ZNF750, facilitating R-loop removal over their 3’ ends and thus promoting efficient transcriptional termination and gene expression. Remarkably, SETX loss affects the activation of the proper epidermal differentiation program in vitro and impacts epidermal layer stratification in organotypic human skin. Furthermore, we found that SETX is mutated or downmodulated in SCC and SETX gene mutation is a negative prognostic factor for cutaneous SCC patient survival. Collectively, our results unveil SETX as a novel molecular player of skin homeostasis, potentially involved in hyperproliferative skin disorders.","gpl":"24676","gse":"193205","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2022/01/10","suppfile":"TSV","samples":[{"accession":"GSM5776484","title":"Ker-CT-SCR_rep3"},{"accession":"GSM5776490","title":"Ker-CT-siSETX_rep3"},{"accession":"GSM5776487","title":"Ker-CT-sip63_rep3"},{"accession":"GSM5776476","title":"A253-sip63_rep1"},{"accession":"GSM5776473","title":"A253-SCR_rep1"},{"accession":"GSM5776489","title":"Ker-CT-siSETX_rep2"},{"accession":"GSM5776475","title":"A253-SCR_rep3"},{"accession":"GSM5776481","title":"A253-siSETX_rep3"},{"accession":"GSM5776478","title":"A253-sip63_rep3"},{"accession":"GSM5776483","title":"Ker-CT-SCR_rep2"},{"accession":"GSM5776474","title":"A253-SCR_rep2"},{"accession":"GSM5776486","title":"Ker-CT-sip63_rep2"},{"accession":"GSM5776482","title":"Ker-CT-SCR_rep1"},{"accession":"GSM5776477","title":"A253-sip63_rep2"},{"accession":"GSM5776479","title":"A253-siSETX_rep1"},{"accession":"GSM5776485","title":"Ker-CT-sip63_rep1"},{"accession":"GSM5776480","title":"A253-siSETX_rep2"},{"accession":"GSM5776488","title":"Ker-CT-siSETX_rep1"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":18,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["35235452"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE193nnn/GSE193205/","geo2r":"yes","bioproject":"PRJNA795433"},"200193143":{"uid":"200193143","accession":"GSE193143","gds":"","title":"lncRNA CYTOR promotes aberrant glycolysis and mitochondrial respiration via HNRNPC-mediated ZEB1 stabilization in oral squamous cell carcinoma","summary":"Oral squamous cell carcinoma (OSCC), the most common malignancy of the oral and maxillofacial region, severely affects human health. However, current treatments for OSCC commonly show only a ~60% five-year survival rate of patients with distant metastases, indicating an urgent need for targeted treatments for patients with advanced metastases. Here, we report a survival-related long non-coding RNA, CYTOR, which is highly expressed in the lesions of oral cancer patients. We found that CYTOR can promote both migration and invasion in oral cancer cells as well as the epithelial-mesenchymal transition (EMT). RNA-sequencing of CYTOR-knockdown oral cancer cells revealed that CYTOR can regulate mitochondrial respiration and RNA splicing. Mechanistically, we found that nuclear-localized CYTOR interacts with HNRNPC, resulting in stabilization of ZEB1 mRNAs by inhibiting the nondegradative ubiquitination of HNRNPC. By synthesizing CYTOR-targeting small interfering RNAs (siRNAs) encapsulated in Nanoscale Metal Organic Frameworks (NMOFs), we demonstrate the targeted suppression of CYTOR to inhibit invasion and metastasis of oral cancer cells in a nude mouse model. Cumulatively, this study reveals the potential role of the CYTOR-HNRNPC-ZEB1 axis in regulating mitochondrial metabolism and glycolysis of oral cancer cells, and illustrates the effective use of lncRNA targeting in anti-metastatic cancer therapies.","gpl":"24676","gse":"193143","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2022/01/09","suppfile":"XLS","samples":[{"accession":"GSM5774540","title":"Cal27-CYTOR-NC-1"},{"accession":"GSM5774546","title":"Cal27-HNRNPC-NC-1"},{"accession":"GSM5774543","title":"Cal27-CYTOR-KD-1"},{"accession":"GSM5774549","title":"Cal27-HNRNPC-KD-1"},{"accession":"GSM5774552","title":"Cal27-RIP-seq-HNRNPC-input"},{"accession":"GSM5774548","title":"Cal27-HNRNPC-NC-3"},{"accession":"GSM5774545","title":"Cal27-CYTOR-KD-3"},{"accession":"GSM5774551","title":"Cal27-HNRNPC-KD-3"},{"accession":"GSM5774553","title":"Cal27-RIP-seq-HNRNPC-IP"},{"accession":"GSM5774542","title":"Cal27-CYTOR-NC-3"},{"accession":"GSM5774541","title":"Cal27-CYTOR-NC-2"},{"accession":"GSM5774547","title":"Cal27-HNRNPC-NC-2"},{"accession":"GSM5774544","title":"Cal27-CYTOR-KD-2"},{"accession":"GSM5774550","title":"Cal27-HNRNPC-KD-2"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":14,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["35963855"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE193nnn/GSE193143/","geo2r":"yes","bioproject":"PRJNA795125"},"200170673":{"uid":"200170673","accession":"GSE170673","gds":"","title":"PD-L1 AND ICOSL DISCRIMINATE HUMAN SECRETORY AND HELPER DENDRITIC CELLS IN CANCER, ALLERGY AND AUTOIMMUNITY [CITE-seq]","summary":"In this study, we define a novel phenotypic and functional dichotomy of human dendritic cells (DC), with strong relevance to cancer, allergy, and autoimmunity. Using 16 different stimuli in vitro (130 observations), we describe two states of human activated DC. PD-L1highICOSLlow Secretory DC produced large amounts of inflammatory cytokines and chemokines but induced very low levels of T helper (Th) cytokines following DC-T co-culture; conversely PD-L1lowICOSLhigh Helper DC produced low levels of secreted factors but induced high levels and a broad range of Th cytokines. Secretory DC were aligned with mature migratory LAMP3+ DC in three different cancer types, atopic dermatitis, and lupus nephritis by single-cell transcriptomics. They were associated with T cell inflammation, overexpressed stimulatory and inhibitory checkpoints, acquired increased cell-cell communication, and were associated with good prognosis in head and neck squamous cell carcinoma, and with response to checkpoint blockade in Melanoma. This functional dichotomy of DC has broad implications in inflammation and immunotherapy.","gpl":"16791;24676","gse":"170673","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2022/01/01","suppfile":"CSV, MTX, TSV, TXT","samples":[{"accession":"GSM5215216","title":"HNSCC_Juxta_ADT_1"},{"accession":"GSM5215213","title":"HNSCC_Tum_Cite_1"},{"accession":"GSM5215211","title":"BC_DC_Tum_1"},{"accession":"GSM5215217","title":"HNSCC_Tum_Cite_2"},{"accession":"GSM5215214","title":"HNSCC_Tum_ADT_1"},{"accession":"GSM5215212","title":"HNSCC_Tum_Norm_1"},{"accession":"GSM5215215","title":"HNSCC_Juxta_Cite_1"},{"accession":"GSM5215218","title":"HNSCC_Tum_ADT_2"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[{"relationtype":"SRA","targetobject":"SRP312422","targetftplink":"ftp://ftp-trace.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sra/sra-instant/reads/ByStudy/sra/SRP/SRP312/SRP312422/"}],"n_samples":8,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["35418195"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE170nnn/GSE170673/","geo2r":"no","bioproject":"PRJNA717965"},"200169381":{"uid":"200169381","accession":"GSE169381","gds":"","title":"PD-L1 AND ICOSL DISCRIMINATE HUMAN SECRETORY AND HELPER DENDRITIC CELLS IN CANCER, ALLERGY AND AUTOIMMUNITY","summary":"In this study, we define a novel phenotypic and functional dichotomy of human dendritic cells (DC), with strong relevance to cancer, allergy, and autoimmunity. Using 16 different stimuli in vitro (130 observations), we describe two states of human activated DC. PD-L1highICOSLlow Secretory DC produced large amounts of inflammatory cytokines and chemokines but induced very low levels of T helper (Th) cytokines following DC-T co-culture; conversely PD-L1lowICOSLhigh Helper DC produced low levels of secreted factors but induced high levels and a broad range of Th cytokines. Secretory DC were aligned with mature migratory LAMP3+ DC in three different cancer types, atopic dermatitis, and lupus nephritis by single-cell transcriptomics. They were associated with T cell inflammation, overexpressed stimulatory and inhibitory checkpoints, acquired increased cell-cell communication, and were associated with good prognosis in head and neck squamous cell carcinoma, and with response to checkpoint blockade in Melanoma. This functional dichotomy of DC has broad implications in inflammation and immunotherapy.","gpl":"16791","gse":"169381","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2022/01/01","suppfile":"TXT","samples":[{"accession":"GSM5199930","title":"HNC_T_BDCA1posDC_D8_3"},{"accession":"GSM5199927","title":"HNC_T_MMAC_D7_3"},{"accession":"GSM5199939","title":"HNC_B_BDCA1posDC_D9_3"},{"accession":"GSM5199913","title":"HNC_T_BDCA1posDC_D4_3"},{"accession":"GSM5199919","title":"HNC_T_MMAC_D5_3"},{"accession":"GSM5199916","title":"HNC_T_BDCA1posDC_D5_3"},{"accession":"GSM5199936","title":"HNC_T_MMAC_D9_3"},{"accession":"GSM5199933","title":"HNC_T_pDC_D9_3"},{"accession":"GSM5199922","title":"HNC_T_MMAC_D6_3"},{"accession":"GSM5199925","title":"HNC_T_BDCA1posDC_D7_3"},{"accession":"GSM5199931","title":"HNC_T_CD14posDC_D8_3"},{"accession":"GSM5199928","title":"HNC_T_pDC_D8_3"},{"accession":"GSM5199911","title":"HNC_T_pDC_D4_3"},{"accession":"GSM5199914","title":"HNC_T_CD14posDC_D4_3"},{"accession":"GSM5199920","title":"HNC_T_pDC_D6_3"},{"accession":"GSM5199934","title":"HNC_T_BDCA1posDC_D9_3"},{"accession":"GSM5199926","title":"HNC_T_CD14posDC_D7_3"},{"accession":"GSM5199937","title":"HNC_B_BDCA1posDC_D7_3"},{"accession":"GSM5199938","title":"HNC_B_BDCA1posDC_D10_3"},{"accession":"GSM5199932","title":"HNC_T_MMAC