:1,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":[],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE163nnn/GSE163872/","geo2r":"no","bioproject":"PRJNA679702"},"200161785":{"uid":"200161785","accession":"GSE161785","gds":"","title":"Gene-expression profiling on a HPV-related head and neck cancer patient with idiopathic CD4 lymphocytopenia and complete remission to checkpoint inhibitors","summary":"PD-L1 immunohistochemical staining has been not always enough reliable to accurately predict the response to immunotherapy; thus, and other biomarkers are required. In this context qualitative and quantitative multiparametric analyses seems promising. Data on the activity of nivolumab in challenging populations (e.g patients with autoimmune disorders; post-organ transplant; chronic viral infections; ongoing immunosuppressant use; elderly) are scant, mainly due to their underrepresentation in clinical trials. Nonetheless, it has been demonstrated that the use of ICI is safe and effective in HIV oncologic patients. Herein, we analyzed a metastatic HNSCC patient with an idiopathic CD4 lymphocytopenia who, after a recovery from a progressive multifocal leukoencephalopaty received 10 courses of nivolumab and obtained a complete remission maintaining  the response  two years after PD-1 blockade withdrawn.","gpl":"23159","gse":"161785","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by array","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2021/12/31","suppfile":"CEL","samples":[{"accession":"GSM4914492","title":"IF71"},{"accession":"GSM4914489","title":"IF46"},{"accession":"GSM4914484","title":"FG61"},{"accession":"GSM4914487","title":"FH44"},{"accession":"GSM4914493","title":"JO12"},{"accession":"GSM4914490","title":"IF55"},{"accession":"GSM4914491","title":"IF64"},{"accession":"GSM4914485","title":"FH29"},{"accession":"GSM4914488","title":"HY51"},{"accession":"GSM4914486","title":"FH39"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":10,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["35083153"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE161nnn/GSE161785/","geo2r":"yes","bioproject":"PRJNA679458"},"200189899":{"uid":"200189899","accession":"GSE189899","gds":"","title":"Comparative analysis of multiple human cell lines reveals distinct stability regulation of alternative polyadenylation isoforms and mitochondrial RNAs in neuronal cells","summary":"mRNA stability plays an important role in gene expression. Here, using 3’ end sequencing of newly made and steady-state poly(A)+ RNAs, we compare transcript stability in multiple human cell lines, including HEK293T, HepG2, SH-SY5Y. We show that while mRNA stability is generally conserved across the cell lines, specific transcripts having high GC content and likely more stable secondary RNA structures are relatively more stable in SH-SY5Y cells compared to other two. These features also differentiate alternative polyadenylation (APA) 3’UTR isoforms in their stability difference in a cell type-specific manner. By analyzing cell differentiation of a neural stem cell line, we show that mRNA stability difference could contribute to gene expression changes in neurogenesis and confirms the neuronal identity of SH-SY5Y cells at gene expression and APA levels. Through comparison of APA isoforms, we show that mRNA stability control buffer the cleavage and polyadenylation site (PAS) choice in the cells studied. In addition, transcripts using intronic PAS are generally less stable, especially when the intron harboring the PAS is large and has a strong 5’ splice site, suggesting that intronic polyadenylation mostly plays a negative role in gene expression. Moreover, we found that poly(A)+ RNAs encoded by the mitochondrial genome are more stable in SH-SY5Y cells than the other two cell types, suggesting that mitochondrial RNA metabolism is distinct in neurons. Together, our results reveal multiple cell-specific mechanisms by which mRNA stability could contribute to the gene expression and APA programs that are important for cell identity.","gpl":"18573;20301","gse":"189899","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2021/12/27","suppfile":"CSV","samples":[{"accession":"GSM5709258","title":"SH-SY5Y_Ft_2"},{"accession":"GSM5709261","title":"Neuron_div35"},{"accession":"GSM5709247","title":"HEK293T_4sU_2"},{"accession":"GSM5709250","title":"HepG2_4sU_1"},{"accession":"GSM5709249","title":"HEK293T_T"},{"accession":"GSM5709246","title":"HEK293T_Ft_1"},{"accession":"GSM5709252","title":"HepG2_4sU_2"},{"accession":"GSM5709255","title":"SH-SY5Y_4sU_1"},{"accession":"GSM5709248","title":"HEK293T_Ft_2"},{"accession":"GSM5709251","title":"HepG2_Ft_1"},{"accession":"GSM5709254","title":"HepG2_T"},{"accession":"GSM5709257","title":"SH-SY5Y_4sU_2"},{"accession":"GSM5709260","title":"hNSC"},{"accession":"GSM5709245","title":"HEK293T_4sU_1"},{"accession":"GSM5709256","title":"SH-SY5Y_Ft_1"},{"accession":"GSM5709259","title":"SH-SY5Y_T"},{"accession":"GSM5709253","title":"HepG2_Ft_2"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":17,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":[],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE189nnn/GSE189899/","geo2r":"yes","bioproject":"PRJNA784969"},"200190224":{"uid":"200190224","accession":"GSE190224","gds":"","title":"Competing endogenous RNA analysis reveals the regulatory potency of CKAP5 in HPV+ HNSCC based on high throughput sequencing technology and clinical validation","summary":"This SuperSeries is composed of the SubSeries listed below.","gpl":"24676","gse":"190224","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing; Non-coding RNA profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2021/12/08","suppfile":"","samples":[{"accession":"GSM5718432","title":"mRNA_HPV-OSCC_C5"},{"accession":"GSM5718429","title":"mRNA_HPV+OSCC_C2"},{"accession":"GSM5718435","title":"miRNA_HPV+OSCC_C2"},{"accession":"GSM5718438","title":"miRNA_HPV-OSCC_C5"},{"accession":"GSM5718434","title":"miRNA_HPV+OSCC_C1"},{"accession":"GSM5718437","title":"miRNA_HPV-OSCC_C4"},{"accession":"GSM5718431","title":"mRNA_HPV-OSCC_C4"},{"accession":"GSM5718428","title":"mRNA_HPV+OSCC_C1"},{"accession":"GSM5718439","title":"miRNA_HPV-OSCC_C6"},{"accession":"GSM5718433","title":"mRNA_HPV-OSCC_C6"},{"accession":"GSM5718430","title":"mRNA_HPV+OSCC_C3"},{"accession":"GSM5718436","title":"miRNA_HPV+OSCC_C3"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":12,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["37664656"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE190nnn/GSE190224/","geo2r":"no","bioproject":"PRJNA786457"},"200190222":{"uid":"200190222","accession":"GSE190222","gds":"","title":"Competing endogenous RNA analysis reveals the regulatory potency of CKAP5 in HPV+ HNSCC based on high throughput sequencing technology and clinical validation [mRNA]","summary":"Purpose: The current study used high-throughput sequencing technology and related basic experimental verification assays to evaluate new pathogenic mechanisms of Human papilloma virus HPV+ Head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (HNSCC) as well as to determine the potential therapeutic targets.  Materials and methods: In this study 3 pairs of tissues were collected from patient with HPV+ and HPV- HNSCC for high-throughput sequencing analysis. After mRNA sequencing and analysis, multiple bioinformatics methods, real-time quantitative reverse transcription-quantitative polymerase chain reaction (qRT-PCR) and immunehistochemical verification assays were carried out to evaluate the potential therapeutic targets of HNSCC (CKAP5). The CKAP5 was then used to further predict the circular RNA (circRNA), long non-coding RNA (lncRNA), and microRNA (miRNA). Sequencing analysis was also performed to further evaluate the potential carcinogenic pathway. Human papilloma virus detection was carried out through Enzyme-Linked Immune Sorbent Assay (ELISA) and the RT-PCR.  Results: Results of high-throughput sequencing analysis, related databases, and verification of qRT-PCR at the cell and tissue level, revealed that the expression level of CKAP5 in HPV+HNSCC was significantly higher than that in HPV-HNSCC. Further, a competitive endogenous RNA (ceRNA) network was also constructed through the analysis of Miranda, multiMiR, lncBase prediction, and sequencing technology. The constructed ceRNA consisted of 1 mRNA, 2 miRNAs, and 1 lncRNA, and 6 CircRNAs. This was verified by RNA22, whereby the relationship between miR-155-5p and CKAP5 was found to be important whereas the hsa_circ_00058495 was a key circRNA according to the binding site scores.   Conclusion: In conclusion it was evident that Hsa_circ_00058495/miR-155-5p CKAP5 may be an important pathway which is involved in the occurrence and development of HPV+ HNSCC.","gpl":"24676","gse":"190222","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2021/12/08","suppfile":"TXT","samples":[{"accession":"GSM5718430","title":"mRNA_HPV+OSCC_C3"},{"accession":"GSM5718429","title":"mRNA_HPV+OSCC_C2"},{"accession":"GSM5718432","title":"mRNA_HPV-OSCC_C5"},{"accession":"GSM5718428","title":"mRNA_HPV+OSCC_C1"},{"accession":"GSM5718431","title":"mRNA_HPV-OSCC_C4"},{"accession":"GSM5718433","title":"mRNA_HPV-OSCC_C6"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":6,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["37664656"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE190nnn/GSE190222/","geo2r":"yes","bioproject":"PRJNA786460"},"200132849":{"uid":"200132849","accession":"GSE132849","gds":"","title":"Identification of genome-wide targets of acetylated NPM1 (AcNPM1) mediated transcriptional regulation.","summary":"We report the first genome-wide profile of AcNPM1 occupancy in HeLa S3 cells by performing ChIP-sequencing using a AcNPM1 specific antibody. We show that AcNPM1 is associated with active transcription. AcNPM1 occupies transcriptional regulatory elements such as promoters and enhancers through its interaction with RNA Polymerase II, general transcription factors, transcription factors as well as histone acetyltransferases and chromatin remodeling complexes. Using RNA-sequencing afer NPM1 knockdown in oral cancer cell line AW13516, we also show that AcNPM1 regulates key pathways associated with cell proliferation, migration and invasion.","gpl":"20301;16791","gse":"132849","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing; Genome binding/occupancy profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2021/12/08","suppfile":"BED, TXT","samples":[{"accession":"GSM3895080","title":"UT rep2 RNA"},{"accession":"GSM3895077","title":"AcNPM1 ChIP rep2"},{"accession":"GSM3895081","title":"Dox rep1 RNA"},{"accession":"GSM3895078","title":"Input"},{"accession":"GSM3895079","title":"UT rep1 RNA"},{"accession":"GSM3895076","title":"AcNPM1 ChIP rep1"},{"accession":"GSM3895082","title":"Dox rep2 RNA"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[{"relationtype":"SRA","targetobject":"SRP201615","targetftplink":"ftp://ftp-trace.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sra/sra-instant/reads/ByStudy/sra/SRP/SRP201/SRP201615/"}],"n_samples":7,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["34898280"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE132nnn/GSE132849/","geo2r":"yes","bioproject":"PRJNA549267"},"200140042":{"uid":"200140